      "dom_id": "question-327532",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A chemist increased the concentration of a lab solution to $125%$ of its original level. An additional reagent then increased that new concentration by $20%.$ If the original concentration was $80$ units, how many additional units were added solely by the reagent?\n\nThe reagent added \n{{field-1}}\nadditional units to the concentration.",
      "worked_solution": "First, the new concentration is $125%$ of the original $(80$ units$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125%\\times 80 & =\\frac{125}{100}\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.25\\times 80 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new concentration is $100$ units. Then, the increase due to the reagent is $20%$ of this amount:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20%\\times 100 & =\\frac{20}{100}\\times 100 \\\\  & =0.2\\times 100 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the reagent added $20$ additional units to the concentration.",
